From: gugu-acp <gugu-acp@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 14:13:43 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] gugu: steer into a cycle the model started on its own

Why: steer() counted a turn as running only when `turnQueue` held an
unsettled host turn. A cycle the model starts on its own (a
task-notification followup when a background command finishes, a peer
message; see AUTONOMOUS_RESULT_ORIGINS) has no Turn, so a steer during it
answered `promptRequired / noRunningTurn` while the cycle was still running.
Gugu then sent the message as a fresh prompt, which the CLI queued behind
the cycle: "send now" became "send when it is done" (gugu#7087).

The CLI itself accepts the message: pushed onto the input during such a
cycle, it is folded into it, and the cycle's result keeps its autonomous
origin and names the message in `user_message_uuids` (read on Claude
2.1.296: steer 3 s into a task-notification cycle -> `injected`, result
`origin.kind: "task-notification"`, the model's reply honours the steer, the
next prompt ends normally). The bridge now tracks that state as
`autonomousCycle`: set when the SDK leaves `idle` while no host turn is
queued, cleared at the next `idle` and when a host turn activates. While it
is set, steer() pushes the message as it does for a host turn, without Turn
bookkeeping (there is no Turn to settle). After a user turn the CLI reports
`running -> idle` even with a background command still running, so the
quiet gap before the notification is not mistaken for a cycle; the case
where it withholds `idle` (background subagents) keeps the user turn held
open, which the existing in-flight path already covers.

Upstream: none yet.

Drop when: upstream steer() injects into autonomous cycles.
